# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the sanitizer job (TSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=...).
#
# Qt is not built with ThreadSanitizer, so the ordering its event queue and thread pool provide
# is invisible: a queued call or pool job, built on one thread and run on another, looks like
# a race although Qt's own lock orders it. Only the frames that build that object are listed. They are on the
# posting thread's stack, never on the stack of the code that runs later, so a race inside a
# slot, a queued lambda or a pool job still reports, as does one on the engine's live data
# (LiveTaps, the rings, the stage meters).
#
# How a pattern matches: by name, anywhere in either access's stack, and also in the stack
# that created either thread. So a pattern must never name a frame that encloses running
# code, or that starts a thread (QThreadPool::start would blind every race on the pool's
# threads). Where our own code hands data across threads it states the ordering itself with a
# release and an acquire (Recorder::post, Engine::startNrBuild), so what a job or queued call
# reads beyond its own captures needs no entry.
#
# Checked against Qt 6.10, the version the job installs, with ignore_interceptors_accesses=0
# and ignore_noninstrumented_modules=1 (set in the job; Linux and macOS default differently).

# The object a lambda handed to QMetaObject::invokeMethod becomes: allocated here, on the posting
# thread. One gap: a lambda invokeMethod runs at once, on its own thread (a direct or same-thread
# automatic call), runs inside this frame too, so a race in such a call is not reported. Nothing
# that crosses threads in this code is called that way.
race:QMetaObject::invokeMethodCallableHelper
# A lambda's captured copies, moved into that object, or into a queued connection's, on the
# connecting thread. Only the constructor: the lambda itself runs in impl(), which this misses.
race:QtPrivate::QCallableObject*::QCallableObject
# The same for a lambda made into a pool job (QRunnable::create): its allocation and captured
# copies, on the posting thread. The job runs in Helper::impl, which this misses. A pool job
# must read a capture to reach its own acquire, so that one read needs this entry; everything
# else it reads is ordered by the release and acquire its code states.
race:QRunnable::QGenericRunnable::QGenericRunnable
# A value copied into a queued signal's event by Qt's metatype system, on the emitting thread,
# then read and destroyed where it is delivered: the EngineStatus a headless run (src/main.cpp)
# and the window receive from the engine's thread. test_recorder runs the app as a child, and
# the child inherits these options. Only the copy matches: the event's allocation is not in it.
race:QtPrivate::QMetaTypeForType*::getCopyCtr
